// Lesson 3.1 — The Turret: Distance & the Detection Radius
// Problem breakdown: locating targets and calculating spatial distance.
// Comparing FindWithTag vs a serialized reference, and Distance vs sqrMagnitude.
// GROUND TRUTH: TurretController.cs is an empty stub BUT it IS already attached
// to Turret.prefab (which also has a child "SpawnPoint" used as the muzzle in 3.2).
// Two Turret instances exist in the Overworld. The turret has NO Animator, though
// an unassigned Turret.controller exists in Artwork.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// Level 3 (outline only): the split and the API are given, the code is not.
// The parts mirror the three sub-problems already named in this lesson's lead
// paragraph and socratic, so the decomposition is the plan the spec below
// follows rather than a separate exercise bolted on the front.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big: 'The turret notices me. That is the whole feature, and "how does a turret notice me" has no answer a computer can act on.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Who is my target, and where is it?',
      does: 'Get a reference to the player from somewhere once at startup, and keep it — rather than searching the whole scene for it every frame.',
      doc: 'GameObject.FindWithTag against a [SerializeField] reference, both in the quick reference, and compared in the reading below.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'How far away is it right now?',
      does: 'Measure the straight-line distance between the turret and that target, every frame.',
      doc: 'Vector3.Distance and sqrMagnitude — quick reference, and the trade-off table below.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Is that distance inside my danger zone?',
      does: 'Compare the distance against a range value a designer can tune, and act when it is inside. Detection is a condition checked continuously, not an event that happens once.',
      doc: 'The [SerializeField] tuning knob you have used since 1.4, and the comparison operators you met in 2.2.',
      href: '2.2.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    '"Notice the player" is not something you can write. Each of these three is — and none of them ' +
    'is about turrets. Finding a tagged object, measuring a distance and comparing a number are ' +
    'ordinary tools you will reach for all year; the turret is just where they happen to combine.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the turret shoot" cannot be. "Get the distance between two objects" can.',
  connect: [
    'In 2.1 you turned "something touched me" into "a coin touched me" by checking a tag. Which part above is that same move — and what is being checked this time instead of a tag?',
    'The spike lesson in 2.2 came down to a comparison against a value: health against zero. Which part above has that same shape, and which value is being compared now?',
    'You have now exposed a designer-tunable number three times: move speed, health, and this range. Which attribute makes a private field visible in the Inspector, and why keep it private at all?',
  ],
  turns: [
    'The reading above compares two ways to acquire the target and two ways to measure distance. Say which you picked for each, and what your choice costs you.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>3.1 — The Turret: Distance &amp; the Detection Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Until now the world has been passive. Coins wait to be collected; spikes wait to be bumped
    into. The turret is your first adversary that <em>decides</em> — and before it can shoot, it
    has to be aware. Today you break "awareness" into smaller problems, read Unity's spatial
    functions, and build <code>TurretController.cs</code> from scratch.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make a turret notice the player?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('3.1', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        The three parts above each have more than one valid tool. Read both sides of each
        comparison before you choose — you are picking on purpose, and you will be asked to
        defend the pick.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Comparing Targeting &amp; Distance Functions</h3>
        <p><strong>Sub-problem 1: locating the target.</strong> Two ways to get a reference to the player:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Pros and cons</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GameObject.FindWithTag</code></td>
                <td><code>GameObject.FindWithTag("Player")</code></td>
                <td>Searches the whole scene by tag. Flexible — it finds things you never wired up — but scanning every object in the scene is expensive if you do it every frame.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A <code>[SerializeField]</code> reference</td>
                <td><code>[SerializeField] private Transform playerTransform;</code></td>
                <td>Wired directly in the Inspector. Zero search cost, and you can see what it points at. But it needs manual wiring, and it breaks for anything that spawns at runtime.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <p><strong>Sub-problem 2: measuring distance.</strong> Two ways to ask "how far?":</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Function</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Why you would pick it</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>Vector3.Distance</code></td>
                <td><code>Vector3.Distance(posA, posB)</code></td>
                <td>The true straight-line distance. Readable, and the number means what you think it means — useful when you want to <em>display</em> it or compare it to a designer's tuning value.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>sqrMagnitude</code></td>
                <td><code>(posB - posA).sqrMagnitude &lt;= range * range</code></td>
                <td>Compares <em>squared</em> distances, skipping the square root entirely. Cheaper — which matters when hundreds of enemies all ask at once.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>If you use <code>FindWithTag</code>, why call it once in <code>Start()</code> and store the result, rather than calling it every frame in <code>Update()</code>?</li>
            <li>Why does the <code>sqrMagnitude</code> version compare against <code>range * range</code> instead of <code>range</code>? What would go wrong if you compared it to plain <code>range</code>?</li>
            <li>You have two turrets in this scene and one player. Which targeting approach would you choose, and what does your choice cost you?</li>
            <li>If <code>FindWithTag</code> finds nothing, what does it return? What happens on the very next line if you do not check for that?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="callout info">
    <strong>The habit that saves you here: guard first, then read.</strong> A lookup that finds
    nothing does not stop your script — it hands you a <code>null</code> and carries on to the
    next line, where you then ask <code>null</code> for its position. Write your code so that
    <em>every line below the lookup assumes the lookup succeeded</em>, because you checked it
    did. Two lines at the top of a method beat an afternoon of chasing a
    <code>NullReferenceException</code> from three methods away.
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — find your turret.</strong> The Overworld scene contains
        <strong>two</strong> Turret instances. Pick one and note where it is, because in 3.2 you
        will need to know which one you are working on. Open the
        <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret</code> prefab and read its Inspector.
    </p>
    <p>
        You will find that an empty <code>Turret Controller</code> component is
        <em>already attached</em> — the same situation as your <code>PlayerController</code> in
        Unit 1. The script exists, it is wired up, and it does absolutely nothing. Also notice a
        child object called <strong>SpawnPoint</strong>: that is where projectiles will leave the
        turret in the next lesson. Note its position.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for <code>TurretController.cs</code>:</strong>

        <p><strong>The range knob:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>How do you expose a "how far can this turret see" value to a level designer, while keeping the field private? What type should it be?</li>
            <li>Choose a default. What makes a good default for a turret in <em>this</em> world — look at how big the Overworld is and how fast your player moves, then justify your number.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Acquiring the target:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which component on a GameObject holds its coordinates in world space?</li>
            <li>Which of the two targeting approaches from the docs did you choose? Write down why.</li>
            <li>Which lifecycle method should hold the lookup so it runs exactly once?</li>
            <li>What does <code>FindWithTag</code> return when nothing matches, and what safety check must follow it?</li>
            <li>What tag does the player carry? Where can you verify that rather than assume it?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Evaluating distance every frame:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What guard clause should stop the rest of <code>Update()</code> from running when you have no target?</li>
            <li>How do you measure the distance between the turret and the player? Which of the two distance methods did you choose, and what does your choice cost?</li>
            <li>What condition decides the player has entered the danger zone? Be careful about which comparison operator you use — you will test the exact boundary in a moment.</li>
            <li>How do you report detection to the Console so you can watch it happen with no UI at all?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — test the boundary live.</strong> Press Play and walk slowly toward your
        turret, watching the Console. Find the exact point where the message starts and stops.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Just outside your range: the Console is quiet.</li>
        <li>Just inside it: the Console reports detection, every frame.</li>
        <li>Walk back out: the messages stop.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Notice that detection is not an <em>event</em> — it is a condition being re-evaluated
        every frame. Keep that in mind; it will matter when you add firing.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Testing Range Conditions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Temporarily change the Player's tag to <code>Untagged</code> and press Play. What appears in the Console? Which line of your own code is responsible for the message — or for its absence?</li>
            <li>What is the difference between <code>&lt;= range</code> and <code>&lt; range</code> in your condition? At what exact distance would the two behave differently, and how would you prove it?</li>
            <li>Remove your null guard clause and press Play with no player in the scene. What error do you get, and which line does it point at?</li>
            <li>Set the range knob to <code>2</code>. Walk around. How does one Inspector value turn a sniper into a close-quarters sentry?</li>
            <li>Move your cached lookup from <code>Start()</code> into <code>Update()</code>. The game still works — so what exactly did you make worse, and how would you measure it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Visualising the danger zone</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Right now the only way to know a turret's range is to walk into it. Read the documentation for <code>OnDrawGizmosSelected</code> and <code>Gizmos.DrawWireSphere</code>. What does each one need from you, and where does the method belong? Build it yourself — do not look for a snippet to paste.</li>
            <li>With your gizmo working, select the turret in the Scene view. Why is a tool that only draws in the <em>editor</em> invaluable to a level designer?</li>
            <li>Tune your range knob using the gizmo as your guide. What radius is challenging without being unfair in a world this size? Write down your number and your reasoning.</li>
            <li>Open <code>Assets/Artwork/Animations/Top_Down/EnemyTurret/</code> and look at <code>Turret.controller</code>. It is a complete animation controller for this turret, and it is <strong>not assigned to anything</strong>. What would you have to do to make the turret animate? Would it need any parameters, and how would you find out?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Break "the turret notices you" into target acquisition, distance measurement, and a selection condition</li>
            <li>Say, for each of those parts, where you would look it up without asking a person</li>
            <li>Explain why caching a reference in <code>Start()</code> beats searching every frame</li>
            <li>Compare <code>FindWithTag</code> against a serialized reference, and say which you chose and why</li>
            <li>Compare <code>Vector3.Distance</code> against <code>sqrMagnitude</code>, and explain why the squared version compares against <code>range * range</code></li>
            <li>Explain what <code>FindWithTag</code> returns when it fails, and why the null guard matters</li>
            <li>Build an editor gizmo from the documentation</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — every question this course answers, in the words you would search with</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — distance, targeting, and the projectile lifecycle</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — <code>Vector3.Distance</code> and Transform APIs</li>
    </ul>
